WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.754
Load balancers, container platforms, and uptime monitors ask whether a site is running.

00:00:06.754 --> 00:00:12.445
The Health Checks module answers them with an endpoint in each tenant that enables it.

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:17.209
In this video, we'll enable it, add checks, read the responses, and see

00:00:17.209 --> 00:00:18.570
who may call it.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.587
Each of these hosts sends a probe request to the site.

00:00:26.587 --> 00:00:31.085
ASP.NET Core has health checks to answer it, and the Health Checks module

00:00:31.085 --> 00:00:34.160
exposes them through an endpoint of each tenant.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:39.960
The module registers the endpoint and a response writer, but no check of its own.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.313
With no check registered, the endpoint reports the tenant as healthy.

00:00:44.313 --> 00:00:47.680
It has no administration pages and no site settings.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:52.190
Only a tenant that enables the feature exposes the endpoint.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.894
A probe uses the host and the URL prefix of that tenant, in

00:00:55.894 --> 00:00:59.540
front of the route, as the three addresses on screen show.

00:01:03.811 --> 00:01:05.025
Let's enable it.

00:01:05.025 --> 00:01:09.162
Under Tools, Features, we search for Health Checks and select Enable.

00:01:09.162 --> 00:01:12.141
A message confirms that the feature was enabled.

00:01:12.634 --> 00:01:15.272
The endpoint answers at /health/live.

00:01:15.272 --> 00:01:16.498
Let's open it.

00:01:16.498 --> 00:01:20.914
The page shows Healthy, because no feature has registered a check.

00:01:25.114 --> 00:01:28.165
Features of Orchard Core can contribute checks.

00:01:28.165 --> 00:01:34.094
Redis Health Check pings the Redis server, and SMS Health Check validates the Twilio credentials.

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:38.814
Each is registered only when its feature and Health Checks are both enabled.

00:01:39.564 --> 00:01:42.466
A check of your own implements IHealthCheck.

00:01:42.466 --> 00:01:49.024
Its CheckHealthAsync method returns a Healthy, Degraded, or Unhealthy result, with an optional description.

00:01:49.774 --> 00:01:54.158
A startup class registers it with AddHealthChecks and AddCheck.

00:01:54.158 --> 00:01:58.859
RequireFeatures loads that class only when Health Checks is enabled.

00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:05.784
Alternatively, make Health Checks a dependency of your feature, so enabling yours enables the endpoint.

00:02:09.984 --> 00:02:12.718
By default the response is compact:

00:02:12.718 --> 00:02:15.452
the aggregate status as plain text.

00:02:15.452 --> 00:02:21.520
Healthy and Degraded return 200 OK, and Unhealthy returns 503 Service Unavailable, so

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.454
a monitor can rely on the status code.

00:02:25.204 --> 00:02:29.424
With ShowDetails set to true, the response is JSON:

00:02:29.424 --> 00:02:35.717
the status, the duration, and the name, status, and description of every check.

00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:41.714
All three statuses return 200 OK, so the monitor reads the Status property.

00:02:42.464 --> 00:02:46.481
The Url setting is the route, and ShowDetails picks the response.

00:02:46.481 --> 00:02:51.629
Set them in the Health Checks configuration section or in environment variables, with

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:54.571
a tenant name in the key to target one tenant.

00:02:54.571 --> 00:02:58.304
Reload the tenant or restart the application after a change.

00:03:02.553 --> 00:03:08.097
To see a check report a problem, let's enable the Redis feature without configuring a server.

00:03:08.097 --> 00:03:11.793
Its Redis Health Check is registered, and it cannot connect.

00:03:12.246 --> 00:03:15.951
Opening /health/live again shows Unhealthy.

00:03:15.951 --> 00:03:22.756
The status code is 503 Service Unavailable, which is how a monitor sees the failure.

00:03:24.419 --> 00:03:29.119
With ShowDetails set to true, the same address returns JSON.

00:03:29.119 --> 00:03:35.955
The Status is Unhealthy, and the Redis Health Check entry describes the failed connection.

00:03:35.955 --> 00:03:39.159
The HTTP status is 200 OK all the same.

00:03:43.359 --> 00:03:50.210
The endpoint does not require authentication, and detailed descriptions can reveal the names of your dependencies.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:54.891
Restrict the endpoint to trusted monitors with a network policy or a reverse

00:03:54.891 --> 00:03:57.859
proxy, and enable ShowDetails only after that.

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:03.723
One endpoint runs every registered check, and the module does not use tags

00:04:03.723 --> 00:04:06.407
to separate liveness from readiness.

00:04:06.407 --> 00:04:09.987
For that, add filtered endpoints in your own code.

00:04:09.987 --> 00:04:13.949
A Rate Limits endpoint policy can limit repeated probes.

00:04:15.949 --> 00:04:20.590
The Health Checks module gives each tenant that enables it one endpoint that

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:24.608
runs every registered check, so your monitor can read the result.

00:04:24.608 --> 00:04:26.079
Thanks for watching.

